1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,403
<font color="#FFFFFF">MuSc is a zero-shot method for industrial anomaly
detection and segmentation,</font>

2
00:00:06,483 --> 00:00:11,143
<font color="#FFFFFF">with no training, no prompts, and no normal
reference images.</font>

3
00:00:11,223 --> 00:00:16,815
<font color="#FFFFFF">Its insight: unlabeled test images hold enough cues
to score one another.</font>

4
00:00:16,895 --> 00:00:20,700
<font color="#FFFFFF">Published at ICLR 2024, it tops MVTec AD and VisA.</font>

5
00:00:21,080 --> 00:00:26,421
<font color="#FFFFFF">Detecting defects is a core vision task, but
existing methods are demanding.</font>

6
00:00:26,501 --> 00:00:30,844
<font color="#FFFFFF">One-class approaches need a bank of normal images
per product;</font>

7
00:00:30,924 --> 00:00:34,981
<font color="#FFFFFF">CLIP-based zero-shot methods rely on hand-written prompts.</font>

8
00:00:35,061 --> 00:00:36,978
<font color="#FFFFFF">Many factories have neither.</font>

9
00:00:37,058 --> 00:00:41,900
<font color="#FFFFFF">The goal: segment anomalies with no training,
prompts, or references.</font>

10
00:00:42,280 --> 00:00:44,918
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors make a simple observation.</font>

11
00:00:44,998 --> 00:00:48,565
<font color="#FFFFFF">In a batch of unlabeled test images of one product,</font>

12
00:00:48,645 --> 00:00:52,856
<font color="#FFFFFF">a normal patch finds many similar patches across
the others,</font>

13
00:00:52,936 --> 00:00:55,287
<font color="#FFFFFF">because normal appearance repeats.</font>

14
00:00:55,367 --> 00:00:59,507
<font color="#FFFFFF">An abnormal patch finds only a few, since defects
are rare.</font>

15
00:00:59,587 --> 00:01:03,440
<font color="#FFFFFF">That asymmetry is a signal already inside the test set.</font>

16
00:01:03,820 --> 00:01:07,772
<font color="#FFFFFF">MuSc is a training-free, prompt-free pipeline with
three pieces.</font>

17
00:01:07,852 --> 00:01:11,803
<font color="#FFFFFF">First, local neighborhood aggregation at multiple degrees,</font>

18
00:01:11,883 --> 00:01:15,835
<font color="#FFFFFF">representing each patch at several scales to
capture tiny and large defects.</font>

19
00:01:15,915 --> 00:01:19,867
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second, mutual scoring, where every test image
scores every other.</font>

20
00:01:19,947 --> 00:01:23,898
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third,</font>

21
00:01:23,978 --> 00:01:27,930
<font color="#FFFFFF">re-scoring with a constrained image-level
neighborhood that cleans the final decision.</font>

22
00:01:28,310 --> 00:01:31,174
<font color="#FFFFFF">The backbone is a frozen vision transformer.</font>

23
00:01:31,254 --> 00:01:38,335
<font color="#FFFFFF">Patch tokens from several stages are aggregated at
multiple neighborhood degrees to represent defect sizes.</font>

24
00:01:38,415 --> 00:01:43,822
<font color="#FFFFFF">In mutual scoring, each patch is scored by its
nearest match in every other image,</font>

25
00:01:43,902 --> 00:01:48,440
<font color="#FFFFFF">and only the smallest score interval is averaged,
sharpening the gap.</font>

26
00:01:48,520 --> 00:01:53,860
<font color="#FFFFFF">The image score is refined with a constrained
neighborhood graph on class tokens.</font>

27
00:01:54,240 --> 00:01:59,162
<font color="#FFFFFF">MuSc is evaluated on the two most-used industrial
benchmarks.</font>

28
00:01:59,242 --> 00:02:04,493
<font color="#FFFFFF">MVTec AD spans fifteen categories, ten objects and
five textures.</font>

29
00:02:04,573 --> 00:02:09,249
<font color="#FFFFFF">VisA covers twelve object categories across three domains.</font>

30
00:02:09,329 --> 00:02:12,283
<font color="#FFFFFF">Both mix normal and defective images.</font>

31
00:02:12,363 --> 00:02:15,318
<font color="#FFFFFF">Classification uses AUROC and F1-max;</font>

32
00:02:15,398 --> 00:02:20,320
<font color="#FFFFFF">segmentation adds pixel-level metrics and
per-region overlap.</font>

33
00:02:20,700 --> 00:02:24,457
<font color="#FFFFFF">Results are striking for a label-free method.</font>

34
00:02:24,537 --> 00:02:28,293
<font color="#FFFFFF">On MVTec AD,</font>

35
00:02:28,373 --> 00:02:32,130
<font color="#FFFFFF">MuSc reaches ninety-seven point eight percent image
AUROC and lifts per-region overlap</font>

36
00:02:32,210 --> 00:02:35,967
<font color="#FFFFFF">segmentation by over twenty-one points versus the
best prior zero-shot method.</font>

37
00:02:36,047 --> 00:02:39,803
<font color="#FFFFFF">On VisA it gains over ten points.</font>

38
00:02:39,883 --> 00:02:43,640
<font color="#FFFFFF">It even beats most four-shot methods and rivals
full-shot approaches.</font>

39
00:02:44,020 --> 00:02:46,079
<font color="#FFFFFF">Ablations confirm each choice.</font>

40
00:02:46,159 --> 00:02:52,640
<font color="#FFFFFF">Combining three aggregation degrees works best:
small neighborhoods catch tiny VisA defects,</font>

41
00:02:52,720 --> 00:02:55,136
<font color="#FFFFFF">large ones catch big MVTec defects.</font>

42
00:02:55,216 --> 00:03:00,128
<font color="#FFFFFF">Averaging the smallest thirty percent interval
beats the max or range,</font>

43
00:03:00,208 --> 00:03:03,694
<font color="#FFFFFF">lifting AUROC to ninety-seven point eight percent.</font>

44
00:03:03,774 --> 00:03:08,900
<font color="#FFFFFF">Re-scoring raises VisA image AUROC from ninety to
ninety-two point eight.</font>

45
00:03:09,280 --> 00:03:13,198
<font color="#FFFFFF">In headline terms,</font>

46
00:03:13,278 --> 00:03:17,196
<font color="#FFFFFF">MuSc reaches ninety-seven point eight percent image
AUROC on MVTec AD and ninety-two point eight on VisA,</font>

47
00:03:17,276 --> 00:03:21,194
<font color="#FFFFFF">with pixel AUROC of ninety-seven point three and
ninety-eight point eight.</font>

48
00:03:21,274 --> 00:03:25,192
<font color="#FFFFFF">It adds over twenty-one points on MVTec
segmentation, all with no training, prompts,</font>

49
00:03:25,272 --> 00:03:29,190
<font color="#FFFFFF">or references.</font>

50
00:03:29,570 --> 00:03:34,114
<font color="#FFFFFF">The lasting idea: a set of unlabeled test images
can supervise itself.</font>

51
00:03:34,194 --> 00:03:36,492
<font color="#FFFFFF">By letting images score one another,</font>

52
00:03:36,572 --> 00:03:40,323
<font color="#FFFFFF">repeating normal structure is separated from rare defects,</font>

53
00:03:40,403 --> 00:03:42,172
<font color="#FFFFFF">with no training or prompts,</font>

54
00:03:42,252 --> 00:03:47,060
<font color="#FFFFFF">giving state-of-the-art zero-shot detection that
rivals full-shot methods.</font>
